//BUG="a void value can be bound to a variable, producing `void var0;` in C"
//ERR any

// Not in FEEDBACK.md. `void` is accepted as a type hint and a call to a
// function that returns nothing can be bound to a variable, so codegen emits
//
//     void var0;
//
// and gcc rejects it with "variable has incomplete type 'void'". The compiler
// should refuse this itself — a compile that produces C that does not build is
// the worst of both worlds, because the error surfaces from a tool the user
// did not run.
//
// 05_functions/66e_using_the_value_of_a_void_function.cpl shows that *using*
// a void value in an expression is caught; only binding it is not.

fn nothing x: i32
    ret

fn main
    r: void = nothing(1)
    out "ok"
